WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:06.790
Speaker 1: I have no choice but to admit that
we have a remarkable economic situation right

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:07.790
now.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:11.380
The situation is not that we will never again
have a recession.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:16.350
In fact, we almost certainly will unless something
changes permanently about the type of economy

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:21.210
that we have, at some point there will be
a recession or worse, because that's the way

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.250
that things have worked for a very long time.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.199
But when we look at the latest inflation numbers
and we look at all of the other data that

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:31.980
we have, it is quite an extraordinary situation.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.960
We have new inflation numbers out today.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.500
It's July, so the numbers are from June.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:44.450
And for the 12 month period ending in June
of 2023 and year over year, inflation is down

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.760
to 3%.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.870
3%.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.140
I have been talking about for over a year
now.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:59.120
I've been saying I believe that by August
of 23, inflation will be down to the low fours

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.592
or the high threes, which would be a dramatic
improvement from where it was a year and a

00:01:03.592 --> 00:01:04.909
half ago.

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:05.909
I don't know anything.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:11.380
It was just my sense of the direction things
were going and I actually was too conservative

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:12.909
in my estimate.

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:14.200
Inflation.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:22.579
Consumer prices in June rose at slowest annual
rate since March of 2021, according to the

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:24.759
latest data from the Bureau of Labor Statistics.

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:28.100
Now, these right wingers who just don't like
these numbers will say, you can't believe

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.100
these numbers.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.100
That's not real.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.100
Okay.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.320
Well, when when you like the numbers, you
believe the numbers from the Bureau of Labor

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.610
Statistics and now the goal.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:35.610
But eggs are expensive.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:41.450
I saw the other day we don't judge these numbers
by what one person, season one grocery store.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.520
This is actually a metric that is extraordinarily
detailed and nuanced.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.200
The consumer price index went up 0.2% last
month and that prior year number, it is now

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.990
a 3% increase, a 3% increase as of last month.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.740
We had a 4% year over year increase.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.570
We are now down to a 3% year over year increase.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.700
These are really good numbers.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.819
And it is not just inflation in a vacuum that
looks really good.

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:16.459
And I'll come back to what we mean when we
say inflation looks good, wages are rising

00:02:16.459 --> 00:02:20.629
and are now higher than they were before the
pandemic.

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:22.140
Inflation adjusted again.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:28.980
The detractors, the the MAGA pessimists will
say, well, sure, nominally wages are higher,

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.640
but inflation has been crazy.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.140
No, no, no, no, no.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:37.050
Inflation adjusted wages, real wages are now
higher than they were before the pandemic.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:42.610
If you look at the G7 countries, the U.S.
has the lowest inflation of all, all of them.

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:47.140
If you look at gas prices, gas prices are
now down about a buck 50 a gallon.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.599
The unemployment rate is at 3.6%.

00:02:49.599 --> 00:02:54.629
That is under 4% for the longest stretch in
50 years, 50 years.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:58.730
And of course, they will come back and they
will say, oh, but a lot of these are low quality

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.959
jobs or a lot of people have stopped looking
for work.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:06.040
These are always things that can be said.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.280
It's the way these metrics work.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.360
You can't only say that when it's to your
advantage.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.780
These are the real numbers.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.690
Now, let's talk about inflation a little bit.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:22.129
I want to remind you that the target for inflation
is not zero.

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:25.629
The target for inflation is considered to
be 2%.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:27.080
We're basically there.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.080
We're at 3%.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.230
The target is 2%.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.930
So we are getting down to an inflation level.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:36.269
That is what you would want for what would
be considered by traditional economic measures,

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:38.530
a good inflation number.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.530
Let me remind you why.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.819
And I know that there are people who have
heard me say this before, but I do think it's

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:49.190
it's such an important basic economic concept
to understand that I want to explain it again.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.730
If inflation is really high, we know why that's
bad.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.240
People can't afford stuff.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:00.530
If inflation is 8% and wages are only up 2%,
things become unaffordable.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.090
So people stop buying them, which is bad for
businesses.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.989
So businesses have to lay people off and then
the people they lay off don't have money to

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:08.989
buy things.

00:04:08.989 --> 00:04:10.650
So then more businesses have to lay people
off.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.450
So very high inflation.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.989
It's pretty self-evident why that's bad.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:20.280
Some people might say, well, shouldn't we
want zero inflation or even deflation?

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:24.530
Prices coming down would be so great everybody
can afford what they want.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:25.770
Wrong.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:32.010
If there is a deflationary situation, you
would expect things to be cheaper in the future

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:33.490
than they are now.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:38.990
And so save for absolute necessities like
food, you need food to survive.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:44.100
If you expect prices to be lower in the future,
you would delay economic activity.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:50.460
If I'm looking at a car and I say, Well, the
car is going to be 3% cheaper in six months,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.250
I wouldn't buy it.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:57.070
And it has a similar effect as things being
on affordably expensive, which is you're not

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:58.710
buying and that's bad for businesses.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:01.060
They lay off workers and you have the same
thing.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:07.110
The reason that by traditional economic measures
we say 2% inflation roughly is what we want

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:13.470
is that that 2% inflation is what drives economic
growth and expansion.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:20.729
Now, of course, when we step out of this traditional
economic analysis, we say, well, the planet

00:05:20.729 --> 00:05:23.650
is of limited size and resources.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.050
You can't have indefinite growth.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:27.660
And that is also theoretically true.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.169
And in some sense it's practically true.

00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:34.740
Before we get to that point, we may be off
to mining asteroids or who knows what.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.910
So it starts to get a little theoretical and
philosophical.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:39.780
But that's the reason why a little bit of
inflation is considered good.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.790
I also want to remind you there are many aspects
to inflation that have nothing to do with

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:45.790
the president.

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:49.840
So I'm not here to tell you we're going to
bow and pray at the altar of Joe Biden.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.870
That would be because of what we're seeing,
much the same way that it wasn't exclusively

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.620
Trump's fault that inflation was high.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:00.389
There are many aspects to inflation that don't
relate to the president.

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:06.830
This includes changes to the money supply,
supply and demand shocks like, for example,

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:12.919
natural disasters, COVID, whatever the case
may be, expectations about inflation become

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:14.310
self-fulfilling prophecies.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:15.960
These things are bigger than the president.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.009
And remember, inflation is coming down in
all G7 countries, but it's coming down more

00:06:20.009 --> 00:06:21.700
in the United States.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:29.150
What presidents can do is fiscal policy like
government spending, taxation, etc., and indirectly

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:30.210
money, monetary policy.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:33.229
Remember, the president doesn't control monetary
policy directly.

00:06:33.229 --> 00:06:34.320
The Fed does.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.849
But the president does decide who is in charge
at the Fed.

00:06:38.849 --> 00:06:45.389
So no reason to play political games, no reason
to sugarcoat or to do Sky is falling stuff.

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:47.699
These numbers are what they are.

00:06:47.699 --> 00:06:53.009
They only slightly relate to Joe Biden, but
this is a good sign.

00:06:53.009 --> 00:06:57.740
Overall, doesn't mean it's a perfect economy,
doesn't mean there won't be a downturn at

00:06:57.740 --> 00:06:58.990
some point in the future

